# Documentation First

## Objective

Treat documentation as part of the deliverable: update it when behavior changes, and write it clearly enough for someone unfamiliar with the change.

## Core Principles

- Update README/docs when a change affects setup, usage, or public API — don't leave docs stale.
- Document *why* a non-obvious decision was made, not just *what* the code does.
- Keep code comments and docstrings accurate — a wrong comment is worse than no comment.
- New public functions/endpoints get a docstring/comment describing purpose, params, and return value.

## Requirements

- When changing a documented behavior, update the corresponding doc in the same change.
- When adding a new setup step (env var, migration, dependency), reflect it in the setup docs.
- Keep examples in docs runnable/accurate — verify them against the current API, don't assume they still work.

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

- Changing an API's behavior without touching the README that documents it.
- Leaving an outdated code example in the docs after refactoring the underlying function.
- Writing documentation so generic it doesn't actually explain this project's specifics.

## Self Review

Before considering work complete, verify:

✓ Docs affected by this change were actually updated, not left stale.
✓ New public APIs/behaviors have accurate documentation.
✓ Documented examples still work against the current code.

## Golden Rule

Stale documentation is worse than no documentation — it actively misleads the next reader.
